 TypeScript is a statically-typed programming language developed by Microsoft as a superset of JavaScript. It adds optional types, classes, interfaces, modules, and other features to JavaScript, making it easier to write larger applications and catch errors during development rather than at runtime. TypeScript compiles down to plain JavaScript, which can be run in any browser or Node.js environment.

Duck typing, on the other hand, is a programming concept that does not rely on inheritance or explicit implementation of interfaces to determine if an object has all the required properties and methods for a specific use case. Instead, duck typing checks if an object has the necessary "ducks" (properties and methods) for a particular context, regardless of their type or origin. The name comes from the phrase "If it looks like a duck, swims like a duck, and quacks like a duck, then it probably is a duck," implying that the shape, behavior, or appearance of an object is more important than its official type.

In TypeScript, using duck typing can lead to more flexible and composable code without the need for complex inheritance hierarchies or interface implementations. For example:

```typescript
function doSomething(obj: {foo: string}) {
  console.log('Doing something with', obj.foo);
}

const foo = {bar: 'hello'};
const bar = {foo: 'world'};

doSomething(foo); // prints 'Doing something with hello'
doSomething(bar); // prints 'Doing something with world'
```

Both `foo` and `bar` objects have different property names but share the same structure (a single `foo` property with a string value), allowing them to be passed to the `doSomething()` function without explicit type checks or inheritance relationships.

 To create a Discord bot with a `ban` slash command using Discord.js v14, follow these steps:

1. Set up your project and install dependencies. Create a new directory for your project, initialize it with npm, and install Discord.js.

```bash
mkdir discord-bot
cd discord-bot
npm init -y
npm install discord.js @discordjs/slash
```

2. Create an entry point file for your bot. Name the file `index.js`.

3. Configure your bot in `index.js`. Add the following code to import required modules, set up your bot instance, and log in with your bot token.

```javascript
const { SlashCommandBuilder } = require '@discordjs/builders';
const fs = require 'fs';
const { Client, Intents, Collection } = require 'discord.js';
const config = require('./config.json');

const client = new Client({
  intents: [Intents.FLAGS.Guilds, Intents.FLAGS.GuildMessages, Intents.FLAGS.GuildMembers],
});

client.commands = new Collection();

client.on('interactionCreate', async interaction => {
  if (!interaction.isCommand()) return;

  const command = interaction.commandName;
  const handler = client.commands.get(command);

  try {
    await handler.execute(interaction);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Error executing command ${command}: ${error}`);
  }
});

client.once('ready', () => {
  console.log('Ready!');
});

fs.readdir('./commands').entries((_, file) => {
  if (file !== 'index.js' && file.endsWith('.js')) {
    const command = require(`./commands/${file}`).default;
    client.commands.set(command.name, command);
  }
});

client.login(config.token);
```

4. Create a configuration file named `config.json` and add your bot token to it.

```json
{
  "token": "<YOUR_BOT_TOKEN>"
}
```

5. Create a folder named `commands`, and inside this folder, create a new file called `ban.js`.

6. Add the following content to the `ban.js` file to define the `ban` command. This command uses slash functionality provided by Discord.js.

```javascript
module.exports = {
  name: 'ban',
  description: 'Ban a user from the server.',
  options: [
    {
      name: 'user',
      description: 'The user to ban.',
      type: 'USER',
      required: true,
    },
    {
      name: 'reason',
      description: 'The reason for banning the user.',
      type: 'STRING',
      required: false,
    },
  ],
  async execute(interaction) {
    const member = interaction.options.getMember('user');
    const reason = interaction.options.getString('reason') || 'No reason provided';

    // Check if the bot has the manage roles permission
    if (!(await interaction.guild.members.fetchNext(member.id)).permissionsIn(member).has('ManageRoles')) {
      await interaction.reply('I do not have permission to perform that action.');
      return;
    }

    // Ban the member
    await member.kick(reason);

    await interaction.reply(`User \`${member.displayName}\` was banned. Reason: ${reason}`);
  },
};
```

7. Start your bot by running the following command in the terminal or command prompt.

```bash
node index.js
```

Now you have created a Discord bot with a `ban` slash command using Discord.js v14. Users can now use the command by typing `/ban <user> [reason]` in your server. Make sure to replace `<YOUR_BOT_TOKEN>` with your actual bot token before starting the bot.

 To convert Markdown-formatted text to HTML-formatted text in C++, you can use a library such as `cmark` or `CommonMark`. In this example, I'll show you how to use the `cmark` library, which is a fast, portable, and high-performance CommonMark parser written in C.

First, make sure to install the cmark library by following the instructions on their GitHub page: https://github.com/commonmark/cmark

Once you have the library installed, write the following function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include "src/cmark.h"

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
  struct output : public cmark_output {
    std::unique_ptr<char[]> buf;
    size_t len = 0;

    void append(const char* s, size_t n) override {
      if (len + n >= buf.size()) {
        buf.reset(new char[buf.size() * 2]);
      }

      std::copy(s, s + n, &buf.get()[len]);
      len += n;
    }

    const char* data() const override { return buf.get(); }
    size_t size() const override { return len; }
  };

  struct document doc;
  cmark_parse_doc(&doc, markdown.c_str(), markdown.size(), 0);

  output htmlOutput;
  cmark_render_html(&htmlOutput, &doc);

  std::string htmlResult(htmlOutput.data(), htmlOutput.size());
  cmark_free(doc.input);
  cmark_free(doc.output);
  cmark_free(doc.Allocator);

  return htmlResult;
}
```

Make sure to set up the include paths and link against the cmark library when compiling the code. This function takes a `const std::string&` containing Markdown-formatted text and returns an `std::string` containing the corresponding HTML-formatted text.

Keep in mind that using external libraries may introduce additional dependencies and build complexities. If you want a more self-contained solution, consider using a pure C++ Markdown-to-HTML library like `disconTOC`, which might be less feature-rich but more straightforward to use.

